Write a single self-contained Python script that extracts inline JavaScript from
HTML files and saves each script block as its own `.js` file.
Describe it as follows so it can be rebuilt from scratch. **Purpose:** A
command-line utility that scans HTML files, finds every `<script>` element, and
writes the contents of each one to a separate JavaScript file under a `js/`
directory with a randomly generated name. **Shebang and imports:** Start with a
Termux-style shebang pointing to the Termux Python interpreter at
`/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python`.
Use `from __future__ import annotations`.
Import `sys`, `Path` from `pathlib`, `TYPE_CHECKING` from `typing`, and
`BeautifulSoup` from `bs4`.
Under a `TYPE_CHECKING` guard, import `PageElement` from `bs4.element` for type
hints only.
From a local helper module named `dh`, import `cprint` (colored console
printing), `get_files` (directory file lookup filtered by extension),
`get_random_filename` (generates a random name of a given length), and `mpf`
(runs a function over a list of files, presumably in parallel). **Constants:**
Define `MAX_QUEUE = 16` at module level.
It is defined but not used by the logic, so it can be kept for parity.
**Function `save_script(str1)`:** Takes a list of page elements (the `.contents`
of a script tag).
Build the target path as `js/` plus a random 10-character name plus `.js`,
wrapped in a `Path`.
If that file already exists, print a red message saying it exists and return
`False`.
Otherwise write the elements joined by newlines to the file using UTF-8
encoding, print a cyan confirmation showing the path, and return `True`.
Note that the join iterates over the element list directly; the elements are
expected to be strings (NavigableString), so reproduce that behavior as-is.
**Function `process_file(path)`:** Accept a path (string or Path).
Convert it to `Path`, read the file as UTF-8 text, and parse it with
BeautifulSoup using the `html.parser` backend.
Find all `script` tags.
If any are found, print a magenta message giving the file's name and the count,
then call `save_script` on each script's `.contents`.
Always return `True`.
Re-converting the path to `Path` twice is harmless redundancy and may be kept.
**Function `main()`:** Ensure a `js` directory exists in the current working
directory, creating it if missing.
Get the current working directory.
Read command-line arguments excluding the program name.
If arguments were given, convert each to a `Path`; otherwise call `get_files` on
the cwd with extensions `.html` and `htm`.
Note the second extension is missing its leading dot, so reproduce it exactly as
written.
Pass the resulting file list and `process_file` to `mpf`. **Entry point:** Under
the standard `__main__` guard, call `main()` and pass its result to `raise
SystemExit`, so the script exits with a normal status. **Behavior and edge cases
to preserve:** - Output directory is relative to the current working directory,
not to the input file. - Duplicate random names are skipped rather than
overwritten; a collision prints a message and does not raise. - Files are read
with UTF-8; no explicit error handling is present, so unreadable or non-UTF-8
files will raise exceptions. - HTML files with no script tags produce no output
and no message. - Script files are written without any header or metadata, one
per script block, with contents joined by newline characters. - Console output
uses colors via `cprint`: red for collisions, cyan for created files, magenta
for per-file script counts. **Dependencies:** `beautifulsoup4` and a local `dh`
helper module providing the four named functions.
The script targets Termux on Android. **Usage:** Run from a directory containing
HTML files with no arguments to process all `.html`/`htm` files found there, or
pass one or more file paths as arguments to process only those files.
Extracted scripts appear in `js/` under the working directory. ---
